<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="ch-us-fuel">
  <title>Fuel efficiency of cars in the USA</title>

  <introduction>
    <blockquote>
      <p>"It's ugly, but it gets you there."</p>
      <p>--- Volkswagen advertisement, 1969, at the time of the first moon landing</p>
    </blockquote>
    <p>
      U.S. Department of Energy data on individual car models make it possible to compare fuel efficiency across
      fuel types, drive types, classes, and years.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Questions.</em> Are cars more fuel efficient than in the past? Which cars use the most fuel and which the
      least? How have available models changed over time? How has fuel efficiency changed for specific models?
    </p>
    <p><em>Sources.</em> U.S. Environmental Protection Agency fuel-economy data.</p>
    <p>
      <em>Structure.</em> 43,516 cases and 83 variables for vehicles introduced from 1984 to 2021.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <section xml:id="sec-usfuel-types">
    <title>How efficient are different types of car?</title>

    <p>
      The dataset includes vehicles over many model years. The numbers refer to models evaluated, not numbers of
      vehicles on the road, so one Rolls Royce counts the same as one Toyota Corolla. Only cars are considered here,
      excluding some missing-value cases and specialist small groups.
    </p>

    <p>
      The data include vehicle characteristics, fuel types, fuel-consumption measures under different conditions, and
      emissions variables. This chapter uses the estimated average mpg values and combines 4-wheel and all-wheel
      drive types into one all-wheel category.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-vehcn">
      <image source="images/vehCn-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>Numbers of car models tested by fuel, drive type, and class.</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Diesel models are relatively rare in this subset. Front-wheel drive models are mainly regular-gasoline cars,
      while rear-wheel and all-wheel categories contain more premium-gasoline models.
    </p>

    <p>
      Americans (and the British) measure fuel efficiency in mpg (miles per gallon), while Europeans often use an
      inverse measure in litres per 100 kilometres. The data include city and highway estimates plus a combined value
      based on 55% city driving. The <c>fueleconomy</c> R package provides related data, but only up to 2015.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-vehcf">
      <image source="images/vehCf-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>Fuel efficiency (mpg) of gasoline cars by fuel, drive type, and class.</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Boxplots reveal substantial variation across class and drive type, including high-use outliers among some
      regular-gasoline front-wheel-drive cars.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-vehhyb">
      <image source="images/vehHyb-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>Fuel efficiency of hybrid cars compared with non-hybrids.</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <figure xml:id="fig-vehyr">
      <image source="images/vehYr-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>Fuel efficiency of non-hybrid cars by model year.</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Hybrids tend to achieve better mileage, and the lowest mpg outcomes are concentrated in older model years.
      Interpretation over long spans should account for changes in testing procedures.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-usfuel-luxury">
    <title>Small (?) luxury cars</title>

    <p>
      Very low-mpg outliers in rear-wheel-drive groups include luxury manufacturers that appear in unexpectedly small
      EPA classes. Ordering by mpg reveals unexpected classifications: Rolls-Royce, Aston Martin, and Ferrari appear
      in minicompact and subcompact categories.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-luxveh">
      <image source="images/luxVeh-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>Numbers of cars by class for six luxury manufacturers.</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Lamborghini are only two-seaters in this data and Ferrari are mainly so. Minicompact, subcompact, and compact
      models occur commonly across most of the six luxury manufacturers shown.
    </p>

    <p>
      EPA classes are based on interior volume (passenger plus luggage), not exterior dimensions or weight. This
      long-standing system can produce unexpected class assignments.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-usfuel-model-counts">
    <title>How many car models have been evaluated over time?</title>

    <figure xml:id="fig-swclass">
      <image source="images/swClass-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>Numbers of models per year across car, station-wagon, and SUV classes.</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Long-run patterns include declines in some small-car and wagon categories and growth in larger car and SUV
      categories.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-swsuv">
      <image source="images/swSUV-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Numbers of station-wagon and SUV models by year, arranged by category and size.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      The displays highlight category-definition changes over time, including SUV split refinements and a sustained
      decline in station wagons before and after SUV expansion.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section xml:id="sec-usfuel-model-trends">
    <title>Comparing fuel efficiency of individual models over time</title>

    <p>
      Grouping by model characteristics allows comparisons of long-running model lines in manual and automatic
      versions.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-tceff">
      <image source="images/TCeff-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>
          Fuel efficiency of Toyota Corolla models for manual and automatic transmission.
        </p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Corolla estimates show city/highway/combined separation, modest transmission differences, and periods of both
      improvement and decline.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-mx5eff">
      <image source="images/MX5eff-1.png"/>
      <caption>
        <p>Fuel efficiency of Mazda MX-5 models for manual and automatic transmission.</p>
      </caption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      MX-5 results are less variable, with a marked step-up linked to a newer model generation.
    </p>

    <introduction>
      <p>
        <em>Answers.</em> Smaller cars generally achieve higher mileage. Hybrids and newer models tend to outperform
        others. Classifications can be surprising for luxury manufacturers. SUVs have largely replaced station
        wagons.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>Further questions.</em> How do city and highway efficiency differences vary by class and fuel? How
        strongly does displacement influence efficiency within comparable model groups?
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <subsection xml:id="sec-usfuel-takeaways">
      <title>Graphical takeaways</title>
      <ul>
        <li><p>Faceted displays support multiple informative orderings.</p></li>
        <li><p>Small sets of boxplots are often clearer with horizontal orientation.</p></li>
        <li><p>Angled axis labels can substantially improve readability for long category names.</p></li>
      </ul>
    </subsection>
  </section>
</chapter>
